    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=\\dfrac{bn-a}{a+b}$.\n\nDivisibility makes $k$ an integer; positivity ensures $0\\leq k\\leq n-1$, so the corresponding adjacent coefficients are equal.",
      "worked": "Write $c_k=\\binom{n}{k}a^{n-k}b^k$, the coefficient of $x^k$. For $0\\leq k\\leq n-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{c_k}{c_{k+1}}&=\\frac{\\binom{n}{k}a^{n-k}b^k}{\\binom{n}{k+1}a^{n-k-1}b^{k+1}}\\\\\n\\frac{c_k}{c_{k+1}}&=\\frac{(k+1)a}{(n-k)b}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe coefficients are equal precisely when\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(k+1)a&=(n-k)b\\\\\nk(a+b)&=bn-a\\\\\nk&=\\frac{bn-a}{a+b}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nIf $bn-a$ is divisible by $a+b$, this value of $k$ is an integer. Since $a,b,n$ are positive,\n$$-1<\\frac{bn-a}{a+b}<n.$$\nThus $0\\leq k\\leq n-1$, and the two consecutive terms exist and have equal coefficients.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 96
